meta {
  name: change-email
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/admin/v1/auth/change-email
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{access_token}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "current_password": "{{staff_password}}",
    "new_email": "{{staff_email}}"
  }
}

tests {
  test("responds 200", function () {
    expect(res.status).to.equal(200)
  })

  test("envelope reports success", function () {
    expect(res.body.code).to.equal("00000")
  })

  test("returns the updated staff member, and never a password field", function () {
    // The client caches this record to render "signed in as", so it has to come back.
    expect(res.body.data).to.have.property("email")
    expect(res.body.data).to.not.have.property("password_hash")
    expect(res.body.data).to.not.have.property("password")
  })
}

docs {
  # Change your OWN sign-in email

The address `/auth/login` checks. **Not** `restaurant.email`, which is the business's contact
address and lives on `PATCH /settings` -- the two are kept apart so that changing where invoices
arrive cannot lock somebody out of the panel.

Every role, and deliberately not owner-only: this changes the caller's own login, not somebody
else's. A floor staff member must be able to fix their own address.

Set as a no-op here (new == current) so a full collection run does not invalidate `staff_email`
for every request that follows.

Things worth provoking:

- a wrong `current_password` -> `TX_AUT_001`. Required even though the caller is already
  authenticated, because an email is the other half of a credential: without it an unattended
  tablet is enough to move a login to an attacker's address and then take the account over
  through forgot-password.
- `"new_email": "not-an-address"` -> 422. Shape-checked in the service so the error names the
  problem, rather than arriving as gin's generic 400.
- an address already used by ANOTHER staff member -> `TX_AUT_007`. Checked across every
  restaurant, which is stricter than the `(restaurant_id, email)` unique constraint: login
  resolves an address platform-wide and refuses when it finds more than one match, so an address
  free at this restaurant but taken at another would satisfy the database and still leave BOTH
  accounts unable to sign in.

Changing the address does **not** sign you out, exactly as changing your password does not: the
token carries the staff UID, not the email.
}
